Dear Lena,

I am writing to you directly because I don't think this is being handled at the right level.

Yesterday morning (Monday 28/9) the chiller on the roof of hall B, your number RTM-H-0007, went into alarm again with t=
he same low pressure alarm as in February. Your technician Jakub came in the afternoon. He was polite and he spent more=
 than an hour on it, but he told our shift lead that he is not allowed to work on the refrigerant side and that a certi=
fied colleague has to come. Nobody has given us a date for that. Since then the unit is running on one circuit.

This is the second visit for the same fault this year. In February your engineer added refrigerant, told us the unit wa=
s fine, and left. We were not told there was anything to follow up. Seven months later we are back where we started, an=
d frankly, dit kan zo niet langer.

I want to be clear about what is at stake for us. RTM-H-0007 cools the chilled rooms in hall B (AGF, fresh produce, set=
 point 2 to 4 C). On one circuit the rooms held overnight, but yesterday at 16:00 cold room B3 was at 6.8 C and we had =
to move pallets into B1 and B2, which are now overfull. Our QA has raised a temperature deviation. We have two retail c=
ustomers auditing us in October and they will look at those logs.

What I need from Corvane:
1. A root cause. Not "low refrigerant", but why it is low, or why the alarm keeps coming back.
2. A date when a certified engineer will be on site. This week, please.
3. Confirmation that you will not simply top it up again.

I am on site all week. Please call me if that is quicker than email.

Met vriendelijke groet / Kind regards,

Pieter Jansen
Facilities Manager

